                  <p>
                    Unlearning is believed as a promising way to remove specific knowledge from LLMs. 
                    Despite various unlearning methods for LLMs being proposed, other works show that they can still let the unlearned LLMs output the target knowledge. 
                    In this paper, we explore a fundamental question: <i>whether existing unlearning methods really let the target LLM forget the target data or not via interpretation</i>. 
                    To achieve this goal, we propose UnPACT, an interpretable tool for <u>un</u>learning through <u>p</u>rompt <u>a</u>ttribution and <u>c</u>ontribution <u>t</u>racking, to effectively ''unpack'' the mystery behind unlearning in LLMs.  
                    Particularly, UnPACT can locate the input key tokens that are crucial to the LLMs' outputs without requiring access to the LLM's parameters. 
                    By reproducing and interpreting 7 existing different unlearning methods, UnPACT indicates that these unlearning methods can effectively compromise the LLM's ability to read correct key tokens for certain questions. 
                    Thus, by simply emphasizing the key words at the end of these questions, the unlearned LLMs have a high possibility (from 22.5% to up to 73.1%) to recall the target data again. 
                    Our finding indicates that these unlearning methods are mirages since they only decouple the question's key tokens and the answer's target data rather than really making the LLM forget it. 
                  </p>
